\section{Shift lengths}{
Each period's length is computed from its start and end clock times, adding
a day when the end is not after the start: a shift that crosses midnight.
The length the source stores is kept beside it as \code{stored_length_hours},
and every period where the two disagree is reported with a warning
(\code{creel_warning_design_length_disagrees}) rather than one silently preferred.
A stored length computed as end - start on a clock that wraps (a 19:30-00:30
shift stored as -19 h) has reached published reports; this is the check
that catches it. \code{crosses_midnight} marks the shifts that make a night
design (see \code{\link[tidycreel:creel_design]{tidycreel::creel_design()}}).

These are the \strong{real} shift lengths. tidycreel divides by \code{p_period}
itself, so a length already divided by the probability must never be passed
to \code{\link[tidycreel:add_counts]{tidycreel::add_counts()}}.
